WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.302 --> 00:00:05.760
GUILLAUME LAFORGE:
Hello, everyone,

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:09.000
and welcome to this session
about the new Java 11

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.460
runtime for Cloud functions.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:13.590
Java is a very important
and widely used

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:15.720
programming language
in the enterprise.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:18.150
And customers have
asked for Java support

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:22.050
ever since we released the
first runtime for Google Cloud

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:23.010
Functions.

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:27.300
So let's dive into Java
11 for Cloud functions.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.250
And that's my pleasure
to introduce you

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:31.800
to my colleague Eamonn.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:33.570
EAMONN MCMANUS: Hi,
I'm Eamonn McManus.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:35.985
I'm the lead developer
for GCF Java.

00:00:35.985 --> 00:00:37.860
GUILLAUME LAFORGE: And
I'm Guillaume Laforge.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.110
I'm a Developer Advocate
for Google Cloud.

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:42.660
And I'm focusing on our
serverless solutions.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:45.750
I also contributed to the
Java API for this runtime.

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:51.460
So I'm very excited to
share that with you.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:55.418
So let's start with a few words
about the serverless concept.

00:00:55.418 --> 00:00:56.960
More than just a
buzzword, serverless

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:02.110
is both about an operational
and a programming perspective.

00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:04.410
From an operational
standpoint, developers

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:06.870
can focus on their
code, and don't

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:11.475
need to bother about servers to
manage, clusters to provision.

00:01:11.475 --> 00:01:13.090
It's a pay for usage model.

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:15.630
So you pay proportionately
to the number

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:19.810
of times your functions are
invoked or the duration.

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:23.370
So you really pay only for
the underlying infrastructure

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:28.290
when it's actually used for your
workloads, not when it's idle.

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:30.360
For the programming
model, developers

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:33.630
are encouraged to write more
granular services instead

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:36.270
of the good old big monolith.

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:40.560
And functions react to events,
so whether it's an http call

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.610
or it's some event coming from
your Cloud infrastructure.

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:47.100
Furthermore, we've been
building Cloud Functions

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:48.940
with an open approach.

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:51.120
So we'll tell you more about
this with the Functions

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:52.330
framework.

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:55.230
And you can easily run
your functions elsewhere,

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:59.580
not just in the Cloud, but also
on your local machine, which

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:02.250
improves the developer
experience, as well as anywhere

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:08.729
else, on premises, or even
in other clouds, potentially.

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:12.125
So Google Cloud offers three
serverless compute solutions.

00:02:12.125 --> 00:02:13.500
So there's Cloud
Functions, which

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:17.280
is the topic of the day, where
you write event-driven services

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:18.980
a small unit of code.

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:21.180
There's App Engine
for deploying your web

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:24.690
application, your services,
your backend APIs,

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:27.150
like mobile backends.

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:29.340
And there's Cloud Run, where
you can deploy anything

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:31.590
within a container
with any language

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:36.830
that you want any runtime, any
executable you want to use.

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:39.160
Now, zooming back on
to Cloud Functions,

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:42.520
there were so far only
three runtimes available.

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:45.660
So there's node
js, Python, and Go.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.900
And today, we're also happy
to have Java 11, which

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:52.660
is available in beta.

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:56.450
Let's now have a look at the
types and shape of functions.

00:02:56.450 --> 00:03:00.263
And I'll let Eamonn tell you
all about our Java function API.

00:03:00.263 --> 00:03:01.680
EAMONN MCMANUS:
Thanks, Guillaume.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:04.277
So there's basically
two kinds of functions.

00:03:04.277 --> 00:03:06.360
And this is true for all
of the different language

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:07.910
runtimes on GCF.

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:10.320
There are http functions
and background functions.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:15.660
So http functions react
to a URL being accessed.

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:18.310
So it's a public URL
subject to access controls.

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:21.390
And whenever there's an http
request made to that URL,

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:22.950
the function gets executed.

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:26.580
And it can specify what the
reply to the http request

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:27.900
is going to be.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:29.880
And then there's
background functions.

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:32.340
And those are invoked by
the system in response

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:33.280
to certain events.

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:36.490
And so we see here some of
the events that that might be.

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:40.010
For example, Pub/Sub
events or a file

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:41.790
gets created in Cloud
storage, and so on.

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:48.620
So, Java doesn't really have
a notion of functions as such.

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:52.210
So the approach that we
chose was a familiar one

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:54.550
for Java, which is to
define an interface that

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:55.720
has a single method.

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:57.580
And then your function
is an implementation

00:03:57.580 --> 00:03:58.510
of that interface.

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:01.220
So it has that method in it.

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:03.700
So when we look
at http functions,

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:06.803
the interface in question
is called http function.

00:04:06.803 --> 00:04:08.470
Typically, you would
just type something

00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:12.355
like, in your IDE class example,
implements http function.

00:04:12.355 --> 00:04:14.230
And then you would ask,
basically get the IDE

00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:15.520
to fill in the details.

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:17.769
It knows that that interface
has one method so it

00:04:17.769 --> 00:04:19.100
provides that method for you.

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:21.100
You don't have to remember
the method signature.

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:23.440
And then all you need to do
is implement that method.

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:24.795
And it's unsurprising.

00:04:24.795 --> 00:04:26.170
There is a request
parameter that

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:29.350
allows you to see the details
of the incoming http request,

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:32.620
like what the URL was, the query
parameters, if there's a body

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:33.988
and what that was.

00:04:33.988 --> 00:04:35.530
And then there's a
response parameter

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:38.080
that allows you to say what
the http response is going

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:43.030
to look like, status code, the
content type, the body of that,

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:44.390
and so on.

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:45.890
GUILLAUME LAFORGE:
Let's have a look

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:50.450
at a slightly more complex
function, an http function.

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:54.220
So I'm defining a new
class in a package.

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:57.550
I'm importing the key
functions framework

00:04:57.550 --> 00:05:02.560
APIs, the http function
interface, the http request

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:04.220
and response.

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:09.100
And in this example, we're going
to check whether a number is

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:10.640
prime or not.

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:13.000
So we're going to use a
[INAUDIBLE] dependency.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:17.630
Here, we're going to use apache
commons math, particularly

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:22.600
the prime's class, which
gives us the ability to know

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:23.840
if a number is prime or not.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:27.970
So, let's implement the
http function interface

00:05:27.970 --> 00:05:29.920
and overwrite the
service method.

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:36.400
The service method takes an http
request and an http response.

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:38.740
Here you can see on
the response object.

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:42.260
I can, for example, set
the status code 200,

00:05:42.260 --> 00:05:43.900
set the content type.

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:46.930
Here, it's just some plain text.

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:50.950
And I can also get the writer
to write on the output.

00:05:50.950 --> 00:05:53.230
On the request, on
the request object,

00:05:53.230 --> 00:05:55.720
I can call get first
query parameter

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:57.430
to get the query parameters.

00:05:57.430 --> 00:06:02.830
In this case, that's the number
that is passed as a parameter.

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:07.450
If there's no number, you
don't necessarily know.

00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:10.730
So this method actually
returns an optional.

00:06:10.730 --> 00:06:13.180
So if there's a number
that is present,

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:15.010
then I can have some logic.

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:19.520
And otherwise, I'm going to say,
OK, please provide a number.

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:24.730
So if a number is provided, I'm
going to look at this number.

00:06:24.730 --> 00:06:26.050
It's actually a string.

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:27.750
It's part of the query string.

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:32.350
So I'm going to turn that string
into an integer in the end

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:34.280
value variable.

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:38.210
And then I'm going to say, OK,
is this value a prime number?

00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:43.960
And then I call the dependency
from the primes class.

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:46.130
Primes is prime with the values.

00:06:46.130 --> 00:06:49.690
It's going to return a Boolean
value, whether it's prime

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:50.840
or not.

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:55.360
And if there was a parsing
error from the query string,

00:06:55.360 --> 00:06:59.360
I'm going to write, please
provide a valid number.

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:01.960
And let's have a look
at the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:04.750
Here I'm building with Maven.

00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:09.190
So I'm saying, OK,
I'm using Java 11.

00:07:09.190 --> 00:07:12.340
And here, you can see
the two dependencies.

00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:14.380
The dependency on the
function's framework

00:07:14.380 --> 00:07:18.640
API with the provided scope,
because the environment

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:23.920
on the Cloud Functions
runtime provides this API.

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:29.500
And I have my user dependency
on apache commons math.

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:33.880
And now let's have a
look at how to run this.

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:35.900
So please provide a number.

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:37.430
If I don't provide any number.

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:41.560
So let's add a number here.

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:42.370
ABC.

00:07:42.370 --> 00:07:42.970
Oh.

00:07:42.970 --> 00:07:44.810
Please provide valid number.

00:07:44.810 --> 00:07:48.880
So let's do it again,
but with 1, 2, 3, 4.

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:51.415
So is this number prime?

00:07:51.415 --> 00:07:51.915
It's false.

00:07:51.915 --> 00:07:55.030
So let's say, let's try
it with another number.

00:07:55.030 --> 00:07:55.660
17.

00:07:55.660 --> 00:07:56.800
Is 17 prime?

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:58.790
Yes, that's true.

00:07:58.790 --> 00:08:03.100
So here's our first
http function.

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:04.970
EAMONN MCMANUS: So we
saw http functions.

00:08:04.970 --> 00:08:07.078
What about background functions?

00:08:07.078 --> 00:08:09.370
The way the background
functions work behind the scenes

00:08:09.370 --> 00:08:11.410
is that you still
have incoming http,

00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:13.640
but you don't have to be
concerned with it anymore.

00:08:13.640 --> 00:08:17.380
So the http has a payload,
which is a JSON payload.

00:08:17.380 --> 00:08:19.970
And we can consider
that comes in two parts.

00:08:19.970 --> 00:08:21.492
There is essentially
an envelope,

00:08:21.492 --> 00:08:23.950
which is the things that are
common to all kinds of events.

00:08:23.950 --> 00:08:26.990
And that says what the event
type is, what the timestamp is,

00:08:26.990 --> 00:08:27.502
and so on.

00:08:27.502 --> 00:08:28.960
And then there's
the content, which

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:31.600
is the things that are specific
to the particular event

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:33.190
that you're receiving.

00:08:33.190 --> 00:08:36.590
Then there are two
ways of handling these.

00:08:36.590 --> 00:08:38.150
They're quite similar.

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:43.010
They just have to do with how
the JSON is presented to you.

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:48.062
So in both cases, the envelope
will be parsed for you.

00:08:48.062 --> 00:08:50.270
You don't have to see how
that's represented in JSON.

00:08:50.270 --> 00:08:52.950
That's the second parameter
to these functions.

00:08:52.950 --> 00:08:56.600
The first parameter, then,
is where they differ.

00:08:56.600 --> 00:09:00.080
In the background function case,
the interface call background

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:04.280
function, that parameter will be
deserialized into a Java class

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:06.470
that you provide using
the JSON framework.

00:09:06.470 --> 00:09:08.660
So that makes it a
little more convenient

00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:11.260
to get out the
contents of the JSON.

00:09:11.260 --> 00:09:13.340
And then in the
second form, we just

00:09:13.340 --> 00:09:16.340
give you a string, which
is the incoming JSON as is.

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:18.980
And then you can parse that,
using whatever framework

00:09:18.980 --> 00:09:21.210
you like, Jackson or whatever.

00:09:21.210 --> 00:09:25.260
So we've seen how
to write functions.

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:30.620
Now, Guillaume will talk about
how you build and deploy them.

00:09:30.620 --> 00:09:33.310
GUILLAUME LAFORGE: So let's
see how we can do that.

00:09:33.310 --> 00:09:36.070
There are a couple of options
for building functions.

00:09:36.070 --> 00:09:38.080
Your functions can
be built in the Cloud

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:42.790
from sources using Maven
or Gradle build tools.

00:09:42.790 --> 00:09:47.050
Your sources should provide a
pom.xml file or build.gradle

00:09:47.050 --> 00:09:48.680
project file.

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:52.180
You can also build a JAR with
any build tool that you want.

00:09:52.180 --> 00:09:54.580
Either a shaded JAR
with dependencies

00:09:54.580 --> 00:09:56.080
within the JAR file.

00:09:56.080 --> 00:10:00.580
Or JAR archive whose manifest
descriptor provides class path

00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:03.460
attribute pointing at
the JAR dependencies

00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:06.630
in some local subdirectory.

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:09.050
With Maven, to build a
JAR project containing

00:10:09.050 --> 00:10:11.070
your functions,
your pom.xml project

00:10:11.070 --> 00:10:14.520
build file must import the
function's framework API

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:15.780
dependency.

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:18.030
This is actually a
dependency, which

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:21.900
is available on Maven
Central or [INAUDIBLE]..

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:24.420
It's a JAR that provides
various interfaces we've

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:28.380
seen representing functions,
requests, responses, context,

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:30.270
et cetera.

00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:33.260
If you want to build with
Gradle, similarly to Maven,

00:10:33.260 --> 00:10:36.260
you need to have a build.gradle
file with a function's

00:10:36.260 --> 00:10:38.148
framework API
dependency as well.

00:10:38.148 --> 00:10:40.190
So here, the dependency
is played over two lines,

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:43.550
but it's really just
for readability purpose.

00:10:43.550 --> 00:10:44.390
And that's all.

00:10:44.390 --> 00:10:46.940
When you deploy this
project on Cloud Functions,

00:10:46.940 --> 00:10:51.530
the platform will take
care of the build for you.

00:10:51.530 --> 00:10:53.810
You can then deploy
your functions

00:10:53.810 --> 00:10:57.530
from either the Google
Cloud console UI,

00:10:57.530 --> 00:11:00.870
from the G Cloud command line,
or with the Maven function

00:11:00.870 --> 00:11:01.370
plug-in.

00:11:01.370 --> 00:11:04.280
We provide a dedicated
plug-in for that.

00:11:04.280 --> 00:11:09.620
In the G Cloud command here,
notice the Java 11 runtime

00:11:09.620 --> 00:11:13.640
flag, which proves that we're
indeed using this new runtime.

00:11:13.640 --> 00:11:15.650
Also note the entry point.

00:11:15.650 --> 00:11:18.110
It corresponds to the
fully qualified class

00:11:18.110 --> 00:11:20.660
name of your Java function.

00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:23.150
Now that we've seen what
functions look like,

00:11:23.150 --> 00:11:26.650
I'll let Eamonn tell you more
about the developer experience.

00:11:29.350 --> 00:11:31.820
EAMONN MCMANUS: So,
in common with all

00:11:31.820 --> 00:11:34.580
of the other runtimes
on GCF, we have a notion

00:11:34.580 --> 00:11:37.360
of a functions framework.

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:40.790
And that is basically that the
code that we-- the framework

00:11:40.790 --> 00:11:43.940
that we use to run your function
when you deploy it to GCF

00:11:43.940 --> 00:11:48.170
is also available as an open
source project on GitHub.

00:11:48.170 --> 00:11:49.970
You can download
that and build it,

00:11:49.970 --> 00:11:53.120
and you can run your function,
of course, by deploying it.

00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:55.490
But you can also run it
locally on your machine

00:11:55.490 --> 00:11:58.280
using this functions framework.

00:11:58.280 --> 00:12:02.270
So and you can also use
that to package the function

00:12:02.270 --> 00:12:04.700
into a container and
deploy that container

00:12:04.700 --> 00:12:07.770
wherever you like to execute
the function wherever you like.

00:12:07.770 --> 00:12:10.460
So what this looks like
in Maven, for example,

00:12:10.460 --> 00:12:12.428
is that in your
pom.xml, we supply you

00:12:12.428 --> 00:12:14.720
with a plug-in that makes
this a little bit easier that

00:12:14.720 --> 00:12:17.180
is based on top of the
function's framework.

00:12:17.180 --> 00:12:22.220
If you put this extract
of code into your pom.xml,

00:12:22.220 --> 00:12:24.590
then you can just type
on the command line,

00:12:24.590 --> 00:12:25.940
maven function code on run.

00:12:25.940 --> 00:12:28.790
We already saw this
plug-in in action

00:12:28.790 --> 00:12:31.600
to deploy the function with
maven function code and deploy.

00:12:31.600 --> 00:12:33.600
Here you can use maven
function code on run.

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:35.550
And then you can interact
with your function,

00:12:35.550 --> 00:12:37.400
which again, is just http.

00:12:37.400 --> 00:12:40.820
You can interact with it, you
know, either from your browser

00:12:40.820 --> 00:12:43.060
or from the command line
using curl or from tests

00:12:43.060 --> 00:12:44.420
or whatever you like.

00:12:44.420 --> 00:12:45.837
GUILLAUME LAFORGE:
Let's come back

00:12:45.837 --> 00:12:47.870
to our prime number
function, which

00:12:47.870 --> 00:12:51.620
allowed us to know whether
a number was prime or not.

00:12:51.620 --> 00:12:59.340
I define the build file for
Maven, the pom.xml file.

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:02.660
And there's a step that I
skipped, I didn't explain.

00:13:02.660 --> 00:13:04.890
That's this part.

00:13:04.890 --> 00:13:07.730
So this is where we are
actually configuring

00:13:07.730 --> 00:13:11.270
the function maven plug-in, OK?

00:13:11.270 --> 00:13:13.860
We're defining a parameter
for this plug-in.

00:13:13.860 --> 00:13:15.470
say, the function target.

00:13:15.470 --> 00:13:18.180
That's the function we want
to run with the function maven

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:18.680
plug-in.

00:13:18.680 --> 00:13:21.140
That's this prime
number function

00:13:21.140 --> 00:13:25.100
from my com xfm package.

00:13:25.100 --> 00:13:31.910
And if I go into the terminal
and run MVN function run,

00:13:31.910 --> 00:13:36.120
it's going to run my function
locally on my machine.

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:38.660
And it's already
running locally.

00:13:38.660 --> 00:13:44.870
And I can, again, for example,
I can do curl request locally.

00:13:44.870 --> 00:13:47.830
Here, 19 is a prime number.

00:13:47.830 --> 00:13:53.650
1, 2, 3, 4 is not
a prime number, OK?

00:13:53.650 --> 00:13:55.870
So it's very simple.

00:13:55.870 --> 00:14:00.280
Just define the maven function
plug-in configuration here

00:14:00.280 --> 00:14:02.290
in your pom.xml file.

00:14:02.290 --> 00:14:05.350
And you're able to read
your function locally

00:14:05.350 --> 00:14:08.650
in your terminal on your
laptop, on your desktop,

00:14:08.650 --> 00:14:12.760
without having to
deploy in the Cloud.

00:14:12.760 --> 00:14:15.500
Actually, we've been
talking about Java so far.

00:14:15.500 --> 00:14:19.550
But you're not limited to
just the Java language.

00:14:19.550 --> 00:14:23.560
You can also run and use
alternative JVM languages

00:14:23.560 --> 00:14:25.480
for authoring your functions.

00:14:25.480 --> 00:14:29.200
For instance, you can use Apache
Groovy, Kotlin, [INAUDIBLE]

00:14:29.200 --> 00:14:30.830
or [INAUDIBLE].

00:14:30.830 --> 00:14:35.145
Thanks to Java, thanks to
its Java virtual machine,

00:14:35.145 --> 00:14:36.520
Java is actually
a platform where

00:14:36.520 --> 00:14:39.410
you can run alternative
languages as well.

00:14:39.410 --> 00:14:43.660
And you can do that with Cloud
Functions for Java as well.

00:14:43.660 --> 00:14:45.180
So let's take an example.

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:50.100
Here is an http function
example using the Apache Groovy

00:14:50.100 --> 00:14:51.600
programming language.

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:55.230
It looks pretty similar to
our Java example from earlier.

00:14:55.230 --> 00:14:59.640
Same approach-- just implement
the http function interface

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:04.020
and use the http request,
the http response parameters,

00:15:04.020 --> 00:15:06.780
to inspect incoming
request and reply back

00:15:06.780 --> 00:15:08.650
to the calling client.

00:15:08.650 --> 00:15:11.100
That's also the same approach
with background functions.

00:15:11.100 --> 00:15:13.100
When you implement the
background function

00:15:13.100 --> 00:15:16.470
or the raw background
function interfaces.

00:15:16.470 --> 00:15:19.590
So let's see a concrete
example with a demo.

00:15:21.650 --> 00:15:24.730
Let's have a look at
a Groovy function.

00:15:24.730 --> 00:15:26.880
It's a useful
function this time,

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:29.340
not a hello world,
which is going

00:15:29.340 --> 00:15:34.650
to parse a file using the Ascii
doctor format, the file that

00:15:34.650 --> 00:15:38.490
is hosted on GitHub, and
which lists the official Java

00:15:38.490 --> 00:15:39.630
champions.

00:15:39.630 --> 00:15:42.000
So it's a table
with different lines

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:47.100
and showing the pictures,
details, names, et cetera,

00:15:47.100 --> 00:15:48.900
town, country.

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:51.270
This file is using
the Ascii doctor

00:15:51.270 --> 00:15:54.030
format, which is format--

00:15:54.030 --> 00:15:57.660
text format--
similar to mark down.

00:15:57.660 --> 00:16:01.710
Each row for each champion
looks something like this.

00:16:01.710 --> 00:16:02.700
You've got some links.

00:16:02.700 --> 00:16:07.770
You've got, again,
country, year, et cetera.

00:16:07.770 --> 00:16:11.670
And the goal of my function
is to output something that

00:16:11.670 --> 00:16:14.460
is easier to parse for tools.

00:16:14.460 --> 00:16:19.275
And I'm going to output
JSON list of documents.

00:16:19.275 --> 00:16:24.010
So I want to transform
that into this, basically.

00:16:24.010 --> 00:16:27.940
So let's have a
look at the code.

00:16:27.940 --> 00:16:30.540
So here's my Groovy function.

00:16:30.540 --> 00:16:36.240
I'm implementing the http
function interface, as usual.

00:16:36.240 --> 00:16:40.030
I have a service method with a
request and a response object.

00:16:40.030 --> 00:16:43.440
I'm doing things like adding
a header for cache control,

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:45.630
setting the content type.

00:16:45.630 --> 00:16:49.440
And then I'm going to
output some JSON data.

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:55.470
I also have a look at the path,
because I can sort my Java

00:16:55.470 --> 00:16:58.840
champions by year, by country.

00:16:58.840 --> 00:17:02.640
And what's interesting as
well-- look at this and that,

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:04.569
same thing below.

00:17:04.569 --> 00:17:09.240
We're using the
Groovy equivalent

00:17:09.240 --> 00:17:10.890
of Java lambda expressions.

00:17:10.890 --> 00:17:12.510
That's Groovy closures.

00:17:12.510 --> 00:17:16.740
So here, we are grouping
by date, sorting by key.

00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:23.619
That's the name, the country,
and again, the key here.

00:17:23.619 --> 00:17:26.880
So this is Groovy
notation, basically, right?

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:28.620
And what else?

00:17:28.620 --> 00:17:34.750
Easily, I can fetch the document
that I'm going to parse.

00:17:34.750 --> 00:17:38.160
And then I'm using the
Ascii doctor j library

00:17:38.160 --> 00:17:40.020
to parse the document.

00:17:40.020 --> 00:17:43.560
And I go through the
various blocks of text

00:17:43.560 --> 00:17:47.670
to create champions,
instances of champions,

00:17:47.670 --> 00:17:50.790
which have a name, Twitter
alias, town, country,

00:17:50.790 --> 00:17:51.330
et cetera.

00:17:51.330 --> 00:17:53.290
So how do I do that?

00:17:53.290 --> 00:17:55.980
So let's have a look
at my pom.xml file.

00:17:55.980 --> 00:17:59.100
This time, I add a
new plug-in, that's

00:17:59.100 --> 00:18:02.460
the G Maven plus plug-in, which
is used to compile Groovy code.

00:18:02.460 --> 00:18:05.770
You have to instruct
your Maven build to say,

00:18:05.770 --> 00:18:09.450
OK, you also want to compile
some Groovy code along

00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:12.310
with Java code potentially.

00:18:12.310 --> 00:18:14.010
So that's the first plug-in.

00:18:14.010 --> 00:18:17.910
The other plug-in, that's the
Google Cloud Functions Maven

00:18:17.910 --> 00:18:19.050
plug-in.

00:18:19.050 --> 00:18:21.900
And I'm saying, OK, this
is the function to run,

00:18:21.900 --> 00:18:24.360
if I want to run the
function locally.

00:18:24.360 --> 00:18:26.250
Let's have a look at
the dependencies now.

00:18:26.250 --> 00:18:29.370
Still, the functions
framework API,

00:18:29.370 --> 00:18:31.470
which is a provided dependency.

00:18:31.470 --> 00:18:33.630
But, since I'm using
the Groovy language,

00:18:33.630 --> 00:18:36.900
I'm adding the
Groovy library here.

00:18:36.900 --> 00:18:41.040
And I also add the
Ascii doctor j library

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:43.698
for parsing the document.

00:18:43.698 --> 00:18:45.240
What's interesting
with this function

00:18:45.240 --> 00:18:49.230
is that actually, this is a
Groovy function using some Java

00:18:49.230 --> 00:18:52.500
libraries, but also Ascii
doctor j, which is actually

00:18:52.500 --> 00:18:55.770
a ruby library, which is
interpreted with the J

00:18:55.770 --> 00:18:57.880
ruby alternative JVM language.

00:18:57.880 --> 00:19:00.480
So you have basically
three languages.

00:19:00.480 --> 00:19:04.140
Java, Groovy, Ruby at
the same time running

00:19:04.140 --> 00:19:05.850
with Cloud Functions for Java.

00:19:06.420 --> 00:19:09.480
So let's continue on
this polyglot journey

00:19:09.480 --> 00:19:12.000
with another language here.

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:14.420
Again, that's the same
thing, but this time, we're

00:19:14.420 --> 00:19:16.860
using the Kotlin
programming language.

00:19:16.860 --> 00:19:18.170
That's pretty similar.

00:19:18.170 --> 00:19:21.180
You have to implement the
http function interface, use

00:19:21.180 --> 00:19:23.790
the http request
and response object.

00:19:23.790 --> 00:19:25.540
That's pretty much
the same thing,

00:19:25.540 --> 00:19:28.080
except, of course, that
the syntax of the language

00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:31.500
is slightly different.

00:19:31.500 --> 00:19:32.900
Alternative languages.

00:19:32.900 --> 00:19:34.100
Check.

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:37.790
But you're not locked to the
function's framework API.

00:19:37.790 --> 00:19:41.300
It's also possible to use
familiar frameworks like Spring

00:19:41.300 --> 00:19:45.200
Cloud, Micronaut, Quarkus,
using their own programming

00:19:45.200 --> 00:19:48.080
models to create
functions for the Java 11

00:19:48.080 --> 00:19:49.820
runtime for Cloud Functions.

00:19:49.820 --> 00:19:53.190
So let's have a look.

00:19:53.190 --> 00:19:55.800
Spring Cloud and Spring Boot
have a different approach

00:19:55.800 --> 00:19:58.170
to creating functions.

00:19:58.170 --> 00:20:00.420
In Spring, a function
is a Spring [INAUDIBLE]

00:20:00.420 --> 00:20:03.810
that returns Java
function interface, which

00:20:03.810 --> 00:20:07.800
is parameterized by an
input type and output type.

00:20:07.800 --> 00:20:10.890
Your Spring functions returns
a lambda expression, hence

00:20:10.890 --> 00:20:13.560
the arrow that you see
here, this example.

00:20:13.560 --> 00:20:16.620
But you don't use the Functions
framework API directly.

00:20:16.620 --> 00:20:21.360
You use Spring's
programming model instead.

00:20:21.360 --> 00:20:24.170
In other framework,
this time, Micronaut.

00:20:24.170 --> 00:20:27.200
For http functions,
the Micronaut framework

00:20:27.200 --> 00:20:30.950
actually uses its own annotation
driven programming model.

00:20:30.950 --> 00:20:34.520
So you can see here, we
are defining a controller,

00:20:34.520 --> 00:20:39.350
a path, path variables, et
cetera, reusing the annotations

00:20:39.350 --> 00:20:41.270
provided by the
Micronaut framework.

00:20:41.270 --> 00:20:44.270
This is really just a
plain Micronaut controller.

00:20:44.270 --> 00:20:47.630
But the Micronaut team provided
a specific Cloud Functions

00:20:47.630 --> 00:20:51.320
integration that wraps the
Functions framework interfaces.

00:20:51.320 --> 00:20:53.210
So Micronaut
developers just prefer

00:20:53.210 --> 00:20:56.090
normal a plain, usual
Micronaut controller

00:20:56.090 --> 00:20:59.580
using their programming
model that they are used to,

00:20:59.580 --> 00:21:00.710
which is pretty poor table.

00:21:00.710 --> 00:21:04.160
Because this controller can
run elsewhere in a container,

00:21:04.160 --> 00:21:07.970
as a web application, and
not just as a Cloud Function.

00:21:07.970 --> 00:21:10.400
Let's have a look at
a concrete example

00:21:10.400 --> 00:21:13.256
with the Micronaut's framework.

00:21:14.270 --> 00:21:16.620
So what does a Micronaut
function look like?

00:21:16.620 --> 00:21:18.390
I'm actually going
to use a project

00:21:18.390 --> 00:21:20.340
called Micronaut
Launch, which has

00:21:20.340 --> 00:21:23.750
been created by the
Micronaut team to scaffold

00:21:23.750 --> 00:21:26.980
a new project, a template
for your project.

00:21:26.980 --> 00:21:29.580
So you can select what
you want to create,

00:21:29.580 --> 00:21:31.920
which language you want
to use, which build,

00:21:31.920 --> 00:21:33.880
which kind of testing tool.

00:21:33.880 --> 00:21:36.160
And you can also add features.

00:21:36.160 --> 00:21:38.630
So I already added it initially.

00:21:38.630 --> 00:21:41.730
There's a specific feature
for Google Cloud Function.

00:21:41.730 --> 00:21:44.340
So the Micronaut team
created an integration

00:21:44.340 --> 00:21:46.980
for Cloud Functions Java 11.

00:21:46.980 --> 00:21:50.340
So you can click the Generate
button to generate an archive

00:21:50.340 --> 00:21:52.330
zip file for your project.

00:21:52.330 --> 00:21:54.210
But you can also click
the little Preview

00:21:54.210 --> 00:21:56.370
button to see what's generated.

00:21:56.370 --> 00:21:58.080
A controller.

00:21:58.080 --> 00:21:59.970
There's even a
Docker file if you

00:21:59.970 --> 00:22:02.670
run that, if you want
to run that function,

00:22:02.670 --> 00:22:05.430
that application, that
Micronaut application elsewhere,

00:22:05.430 --> 00:22:07.830
for example, as a
Cloud Run container.

00:22:07.830 --> 00:22:09.060
That's possible.

00:22:09.060 --> 00:22:12.330
And there's also a build.

00:22:12.330 --> 00:22:16.930
But I've already
downloaded this.

00:22:16.930 --> 00:22:21.210
And, so I have a build.gradle
file that's been generated

00:22:21.210 --> 00:22:24.240
using the function's
framework API, the invoker--

00:22:24.240 --> 00:22:28.090
that's what allows you to run
functions locally or invoke

00:22:28.090 --> 00:22:30.360
your functions anywhere.

00:22:30.360 --> 00:22:33.420
And what's interesting
is that there's

00:22:33.420 --> 00:22:36.650
a special task that's
been added here,

00:22:36.650 --> 00:22:40.040
Run Function, which does the
same job as the Maven function

00:22:40.040 --> 00:22:42.330
plug in, which is
using the invoker

00:22:42.330 --> 00:22:44.010
to run the function locally.

00:22:44.010 --> 00:22:47.940
And it's going to call the
integration point created

00:22:47.940 --> 00:22:51.510
by the Micronaut folks,
the http function, which

00:22:51.510 --> 00:22:55.480
is going to delegate the
work to the Halo controller.

00:22:55.480 --> 00:22:58.440
So this controller, it's a
standard Micronaut controller,

00:22:58.440 --> 00:23:01.530
using the Add
Controller annotation,

00:23:01.530 --> 00:23:04.590
looking at the /halo sub path.

00:23:04.590 --> 00:23:07.450
It's going to output
some plaintext.

00:23:07.450 --> 00:23:09.340
Let's write something.

00:23:09.340 --> 00:23:12.490
GCF Java 11 rocks.

00:23:12.490 --> 00:23:13.710
Let's save this.

00:23:13.710 --> 00:23:15.450
And then let's run that locally.

00:23:15.450 --> 00:23:18.870
So it's going to build this
function locally and run

00:23:18.870 --> 00:23:22.220
it locally using the
functions invoker

00:23:22.220 --> 00:23:25.860
through the Gradle build.

00:23:25.860 --> 00:23:27.280
So let's have a look.

00:23:27.280 --> 00:23:27.780
Yeah.

00:23:27.780 --> 00:23:29.330
GCF Java 11 rocks.

00:23:30.705 --> 00:23:33.590
So, unlike Spring
Cloud and Micronaut,

00:23:33.590 --> 00:23:37.530
Quarkus follows the function
framework native API approach,

00:23:37.530 --> 00:23:41.310
just decorating functions with
Quarkus specific annotations,

00:23:41.310 --> 00:23:45.390
like @Named,
@ApplicationScoped, or @Inject

00:23:45.390 --> 00:23:49.050
to do dependency injection
for the dependencies that

00:23:49.050 --> 00:23:51.120
are needed for your function.

00:23:51.120 --> 00:23:54.750
This is also the same approach
for background functions.

00:23:54.750 --> 00:23:57.510
So taking advantage of
Quarkus annotations,

00:23:57.510 --> 00:23:59.370
but following the
Functions framework

00:23:59.370 --> 00:24:02.070
API using the
Functions framework API

00:24:02.070 --> 00:24:04.518
interfaces instead.

00:24:04.518 --> 00:24:05.310
So that's about it.

00:24:05.310 --> 00:24:07.890
I'll let Eamonn conclude.

00:24:07.890 --> 00:24:11.610
EAMONN MCMANUS: So, we saw
a rapid tour of GCF Java.

00:24:11.610 --> 00:24:15.230
So Java is one of the new
runtimes available with GCF

00:24:15.230 --> 00:24:16.590
now.

00:24:16.590 --> 00:24:19.620
We saw that there are two kinds
of functions, http functions

00:24:19.620 --> 00:24:21.360
and background functions.

00:24:21.360 --> 00:24:23.730
Both functions are
portable, thanks

00:24:23.730 --> 00:24:24.900
to the Functions framework.

00:24:24.900 --> 00:24:28.650
So you can deploy them using
the Functions framework

00:24:28.650 --> 00:24:30.750
in production to GCF.

00:24:30.750 --> 00:24:32.322
But you can also
run them locally.

00:24:32.322 --> 00:24:33.780
And you can put
them in a container

00:24:33.780 --> 00:24:35.800
and deploy them
anywhere you want.

00:24:35.800 --> 00:24:38.250
We saw all of that it isn't
really just a Java runtime.

00:24:38.250 --> 00:24:39.420
It's the JVM runtime.

00:24:39.420 --> 00:24:45.070
So you can run any JVM language,
such as Kotlin or Groovy.

00:24:45.070 --> 00:24:48.405
And finally, we saw that it
works not only on its own,

00:24:48.405 --> 00:24:50.280
but also in conjunction
with a certain number

00:24:50.280 --> 00:24:54.450
of other frameworks, so such
as Spring Cloud, Quarkus,

00:24:54.450 --> 00:24:55.540
and Micronaut.

00:24:55.540 --> 00:24:57.150
So that's GCF Java.

00:24:57.150 --> 00:25:00.560
And we're excited to see
what you build with it.

